WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.434
One lecture set

00:00:01.246 --> 00:00:02.449
math's agenda for

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.097
a century

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.776
In August 1900 at

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:06.696
a math congress

00:00:06.507 --> 00:00:07.297
in Paris

00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:08.736
David Hilbert gave

00:00:08.547 --> 00:00:09.266
a talk on

00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:10.045
the problems of

00:00:09.856 --> 00:00:11.130
the coming century

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:12.471
His friend Hermann

00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:13.155
Minkowski

00:00:13.285 --> 00:00:14.169
had urged him

00:00:13.981 --> 00:00:14.924
to look ahead

00:00:15.454 --> 00:00:16.232
He had time

00:00:16.044 --> 00:00:16.928
to read only

00:00:16.739 --> 00:00:17.647
ten aloud

00:00:17.786 --> 00:00:19.024
The published version

00:00:18.847 --> 00:00:20.085
listed 23

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.217
from the nature

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:21.866
of the infinite

00:00:21.678 --> 00:00:22.585
to the stacking

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:23.293
of spheres

00:00:23.822 --> 00:00:25.060
Solving one became

00:00:24.872 --> 00:00:25.767
the surest way

00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:26.109
to make a

00:00:25.921 --> 00:00:27.194
name in mathematics

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:28.303
and whole fields

00:00:28.114 --> 00:00:28.975
grew up around

00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:29.883
single entries on

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:30.437
the list

00:00:30.966 --> 00:00:31.980
Full story on

00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:32.687
the dashboard
